mg-amiga @VERSION@ -- mg @UPSTREAM@ (Micro Emacs) for AmigaOS 3.x
==============================================================

mg is a small, fast Emacs: the same keys and commands as GNU Emacs for
everyday editing, in one program of about 200 KB and no support files.
This is the AmigaOS port of mg @UPSTREAM@ (https://github.com/troglobit/mg),
made for the Shell window: AmigaOS 3.x, any 68000 or better.

Install
-------
Copy mg to C: (or to any drawer on the path):

    Copy mg-amiga/mg C:

and start it from a Shell: "mg" or "mg S:Startup-Sequence".  To start it
as "emacs" too, add this line to S:Shell-Startup:

    ALIAS emacs mg []

Keys on the Amiga
-----------------
    Cursor keys          move
    Shift+Left/Right     start/end of line
    Shift+Up/Down        page up/down
    Backspace, Del       delete left, delete right
    F1 or Help           quick help
    Esc, then a key      Meta (M-x is Esc x)
    C-x C-f              open a file;  C-x C-s save;  C-x C-c quit

Names are Amiga names: "Work:dir/file", "RAM:notes".  In the prompts, a
volume typed after the default directory starts the name over: typing
"RAM:x" after "Work:dir/" gives "RAM:x", and ".." is the parent:
"Work:dir/../x" is "Work:x".  The window size is read when mg starts and
on C-l (after resizing the window).  Text is ISO-8859-1.

Saving a file keeps its protection bits and comment; new files get
----rwed.  The old version is kept as "name~".

Directories
-----------
"mg Work:" or C-x C-f on a directory opens dired, the directory editor:
Return opens the entry, ^ goes to the parent, c copies, r renames, d
marks for deletion and x deletes the marked ones, + makes a directory,
g reads the directory again, q leaves.  The listing shows the protection
bits as List does (----rwed).

Not in this port
----------------
AmigaOS cannot run programs the Unix way mg expects, so these are left
out: shell-command and pipes (also ! in dired), compile and grep, cscope
and ctags, regex search, suspend.  Backspace and Ctrl-H send the same
key, so C-h is delete-backward, not the help prefix (use F1 or Help).
No startup file unless HOME is set (then $HOME/.mg).

